  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/first-import-of-rhadinoloricaria-macromystax/",
      "html": "<p>Even keepers specialized in whiptail catfish will hardly know this fish. Until now only preserved specimens or pictures of animals photographed in the field were known. Now some specimens could be imported alive from Venezuela for the first time ever. The species was described in 1869 from Peru.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/first-import-of-rhadinoloricaria-macromystax/",
      "html": "<p>These fish are extremely sensitive. The supplier reports big difficulties during the acclimatization of the fish. The reasons for these difficulties are unknown. The way the fish hold their pectoral and ventral fins (like spoilers) may be a hint that these whiptail cats are adapted to strong current (a so-called rheophilic species).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/first-import-of-rhadinoloricaria-macromystax/",
      "html": "<p>The species is said to attain a length of about 15 cm. This means that the imported specimens are fully grown or at least sexually mature. Whether they represent the \"real\" Rh. macromystax or a close, still undescribed relative cannot be decided. The genus is only insufficiently researched.</p>"
    }
  ],
